## Definition

Assume the axiom of countable choice \(\mathrm{AC}_\omega\). Let \(M\) be a finite-dimensional, Hausdorff, second-countable smooth manifold without boundary. Fix a compact noncommutative finite-dimensional real Lie group \(G\), whose Lie algebra is \(\mathfrak g=T_eG\) with bracket transported from left-invariant vector fields. Let \(\pi:P\to M\) be a smooth right principal bundle: the right action is smooth, free and transitive on each fibre, and each point has a local equivariant principal chart. Let \(H\subset TP\) be a supplied smooth right-invariant horizontal complement to \(VP=\ker(d\pi)\), and write \(v\) for projection onto \(VP\) along \(H\). For \(Z\in\mathfrak g\), the fundamental vertical vector is \(Z_P(p)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}p\exp_G(tZ)\). The principal connection form \(\omega\) is a smooth \(\mathfrak g\)-valued one-form satisfying \(\omega(Z_P)=Z\), \(\ker\omega=H\), and \(R_g^*\omega=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega\), where \(\operatorname{Ad}_g=d(C_g)_e\) and \(C_g(h)=ghg^{-1}\). In a local section \(s:U\to P\) (a smooth map with \(\pi\circ s=\operatorname{id}_U\)), the potential \(A=s^*\omega\) changes under a section change \(s'=su\), for smooth \(u:U\to G\), by \(A'=\operatorname{Ad}_{u^{-1}}A+\theta_L(u)\), where \(\theta_L(u)=u^*\theta_L\), \((\theta_L)_g=d(L_{g^{-1}})_g\), and \(L_g(h)=gh\). The full gauge group consists of smooth diffeomorphisms \(\Phi:P\to P\) satisfying \(\pi\circ\Phi=\pi\) and \(\Phi(pg)=\Phi(p)g\); this definition gives it no finite-dimensional Lie-group structure.

A **finite-dimensional regulated gauge-slice datum** consists of integers \(1\le k\le n\); a finite-dimensional Lie group \(K\) of dimension \(k\), with Lie algebra \(\mathfrak k:=T_eK\) and bracket transported from left-invariant vector fields; a chosen smooth positive right-invariant density \(\mu_K\), meaning \((R_g^K)^*\mu_K=\mu_K\) for \(R_g^K(h)=hg\); a finite-dimensional smooth manifold \(X\) of dimension \(n\) with a chosen smooth positive density \(\mu_X\); and a smooth right action \(\alpha:X\times K\to X\), written \(x\cdot g\), with \(x\cdot e=x\) and \((x\cdot g)\cdot h=x\cdot(gh)\). A smooth positive density on an \(m\)-manifold has coordinate form \(\rho(x)|dx^1\cdots dx^m|\) with smooth \(\rho>0\), and transforms by the absolute coordinate Jacobian. The group \(K\) and its action on \(X\) are separate regulator data. They are not identified with the full gauge group or the full space of connections unless a particular finite model explicitly constructs such an action and verifies that it preserves \(X\).

Choose an embedded smooth submanifold \(S\subset X\) of codimension \(k\), a smooth positive density \(\mu_S\), and an open neighborhood \(U\subset X\) of a point \(s\in S\). Embedded means that near each point of \(S\), a smooth chart on \(X\) identifies \(S\) with \(\mathbb R^{n-k}\times\{0\}\). A **gauge condition** on \(U\) is a smooth map \(\chi:U\to\mathbb R^k\) such that \(U\cap S=\chi^{-1}(0)\) and, for every \(s'\in U\cap S\), \(d\chi_{s'}\) is surjective with kernel \(T_{s'}S\). The **infinitesimal orbit map** at \(s'\) is \(R_{s'}:=d(\alpha_{s'})_e:\mathfrak k\to T_{s'}X\), where \(\alpha_{s'}(g)=s'\cdot g\); call its kernel the infinitesimal stabilizer. The **Faddeev–Popov operator** is the ordinary linear map \(M_{s'}:=d\chi_{s'}\circ R_{s'}:\mathfrak k\to\mathbb R^k\). It is **transverse at \(s'\)** when \(M_{s'}\) is an isomorphism. Choose a positive density \(\nu\) on \(\mathfrak k\) equal to \((\mu_K)_e\), and the standard positive density \(\lambda\) on \(\mathbb R^k\). The finite-dimensional Faddeev–Popov Jacobian \(\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}(s')\) is the nonnegative density ratio defined by \(M_{s'}^*\lambda=\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}(s')\nu\); in chosen bases it is \(|\det M_{s'}|\), and it is zero when \(M_{s'}\) is singular.

This datum defines only finite-dimensional Jacobians and local coordinate slices. It neither defines a determinant of an infinite-dimensional operator nor constructs a functional measure or a continuum path integral. A finite-mode or lattice application must supply the regulator manifold \(X\), the finite-dimensional group \(K\), their smooth action, and the domains and densities above.

