+## Definition
+
+Use a compact non-Abelian Lie group $G$, its Lie algebra $\mathfrak g$, a local gauge potential $A_\mu$ and the finite collection of matter fields and representations selected by the adopted Yang–Mills model. Work in a local trivialization of the selected bundle sector on oriented, time-oriented Minkowski space with signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and coordinates $x^0=ct$. For the formal perturbative formulas set $c=\hbar=1$; $A_\mu$ and the gauge coupling $g$ have canonical engineering dimensions $1$ and $0$, respectively, in mass units. The model supplies the invariant quadratic form, matter action, physical units before this normalization, and boundary conditions. The adopted model postulate and the classical gauge-invariance result supply the Yang–Mills action and its gauge-invariant interpretation; this definition does not prove either supplier.
+
+In the chosen local trivialization write $D_\mu c=\partial_\mu c+[A_\mu,c]$ and choose the linear covariant gauge condition $G(A)=\partial^\mu A_\mu=0$ with gauge parameter $\xi>0$. The nonminimal BRST variables are an odd antighost $\bar c$ of ghost number $-1$ and an even auxiliary field $b$ of ghost number $0$, with $s\bar c=b$ and $sb=0$. The gauge-fixing fermion and gauge-fixing contribution are
+
+$$\Psi_\xi=\int d^4x\,\langle\bar c,\partial^\mu A_\mu+\tfrac{\xi}{2}b\rangle,\qquad S_{\rm gf}:=s\Psi_\xi.$$
+
+Here $s$ is the odd BRST derivation on the finite-jet field algebra, extended to the nonminimal variables and their antifields using the specified BV convention. The gauge condition is a local perturbative choice; it does not assert a global slice or remove all gauge copies. A finite-dimensional regulated configuration space has an ordinary Faddeev–Popov Jacobian only when it supplies the finite-dimensional group action, gauge condition, and transverse slice data in the preceding finite-dimensional definition. Such a Jacobian is not a continuum functional determinant and does not define a continuum measure.
+
+A **formal perturbative renormalization datum** consists of the selected classical BV action $\Gamma_0$, the above gauge-fixing choice, the dimensional-regularization prescription $D=4-2\epsilon$, modified minimal subtraction of the $1/\epsilon$ poles together with $-\gamma_E+\log(4\pi)$, and a local counterterm space $\mathcal C_4$. The space $\mathcal C_4$ consists of integrated Lorentz-scalar polynomials of ghost number zero and engineering dimension at most four in the fields, ghosts, antighosts, auxiliary variables, antifields, and finitely many of their spacetime derivatives. The datum is admissible only when $\Gamma_0$ satisfies the classical master equation. Its loop-counting variable $\lambda$ is formal and dimensionless; a formal effective action is a coefficient sequence $\Gamma(\lambda)=\Gamma_0+\sum_{n\geq1}\lambda^n\Gamma_n$ and no convergence at any nonzero $\lambda$ is asserted.
+
+For a local BV functional $\Gamma$, define the Slavnov functional by $\mathfrak S(\Gamma):=\tfrac12(\Gamma,\Gamma)$ using the chosen odd antibracket, and define the linearized operator $B_{\Gamma_0}X:=(\Gamma_0,X)$. The datum has the **local QAP property** when, at each first order $n$ where the coefficientwise Slavnov identity fails, its defect has the form
+
+$$\mathfrak S(\Gamma^{(<n)})=\lambda^n\Delta_n+O(\lambda^{n+1}),$$
+
+where $\Delta_n$ is an integrated local functional of ghost number one in the finite-jet BV algebra. This is a stated property of the formal renormalization input, not a theorem of this definition. The finite-jet local BRST cohomology $H^{g,p}(s\mid d_H)$ uses the local forms, ghost grading, and horizontal differential of the preceding cohomology definition; $d_H$-exact top forms are identified with zero only under the declared compact-support or boundary condition. The coefficientwise Slavnov–Taylor identity is $\mathfrak S(\Gamma(\lambda))=0$ as an equality of formal power series.
+
