+## Definition
+
+Use a compact non-Abelian Lie group $G$, its Lie algebra $\mathfrak g$, a local gauge potential $A_\mu$ and the finite collection of matter fields and representations selected by the adopted Yang–Mills model. Work in a local trivialization of the selected bundle sector on oriented, time-oriented Minkowski space with signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and coordinates $x^0=ct$. For the formal perturbative formulas set $c=\hbar=1$; $A_\mu$ and the gauge coupling $g$ have canonical engineering dimensions $1$ and $0$, respectively, in mass units. Give the ghost $c$ dimension $0$, the antighost $\bar c$ and auxiliary field $b$ dimension $2$, and the BRST differential dimension $0$. The matter-field dimensions are those assigned by the model's chosen kinetic terms; assign each antifield the dimension making its product with the corresponding BRST variation have density dimension $4$. The model supplies the invariant quadratic form, matter action, physical units before this normalization, and boundary conditions. The adopted model postulate and the classical gauge-invariance result supply the Yang–Mills action and its gauge-invariant interpretation; this definition does not prove either supplier.
+
+In the chosen local trivialization write $D_\mu c=\partial_\mu c+[A_\mu,c]$ and choose the linear covariant gauge condition $G(A)=\partial^\mu A_\mu=0$ with gauge parameter $\xi>0$. The nonminimal BRST variables are an odd antighost $\bar c$ of ghost number $-1$ and an even auxiliary field $b$ of ghost number $0$, with $s\bar c=b$ and $sb=0$. The gauge-fixing fermion and gauge-fixing contribution are
+
+$$\Psi_\xi=\int d^4x\,\langle\bar c,\partial^\mu A_\mu+\tfrac{\xi}{2}b\rangle,\qquad S_{\rm gf}:=s\Psi_\xi.$$
+
+Here $s$ is the odd BRST derivation on the finite-jet field algebra, extended to the nonminimal variables and their antifields using the specified BV convention. The gauge condition is a local perturbative choice; it does not assert a global slice or remove all gauge copies. A finite-dimensional regulated configuration space has an ordinary Faddeev–Popov Jacobian only when it supplies the finite-dimensional group action, gauge condition, and transverse slice data in the preceding finite-dimensional definition. Such a Jacobian is not a continuum functional determinant and does not define a continuum measure.
+
+A **formal perturbative renormalization datum** consists of the selected classical BV action $\Gamma_0$, the above gauge-fixing choice, the dimensional-regularization prescription $D=4-2\epsilon$, modified minimal subtraction of the $1/\epsilon$ poles together with $-\gamma_E+\log(4\pi)$, and a local counterterm space $\mathcal C_{\leq d_*}$. Here $d_*$ is a finite engineering-dimension bound fixed by the selected power-counting prescription; $\mathcal C_{\leq d_*}$ consists of integrated Lorentz-scalar polynomials of ghost number zero and engineering dimension at most $d_*$ in the fields, ghosts, antighosts, auxiliary variables, antifields, and finitely many of their spacetime derivatives. The usual renormalizable four-dimensional Yang–Mills choice is $d_*=4$. The datum is admissible only when $\Gamma_0$ satisfies the classical master equation. Its loop-counting variable $\lambda$ is formal and dimensionless; a formal effective action is a coefficient sequence $\Gamma(\lambda)=\Gamma_0+\sum_{n\geq1}\lambda^n\Gamma_n$ and no convergence at any nonzero $\lambda$ is asserted.
+
+For a local BV functional $\Gamma$, define the Slavnov functional by $\mathfrak S(\Gamma):=\tfrac12(\Gamma,\Gamma)$ using the chosen odd antibracket, and define the linearized operator $B_{\Gamma_0}X:=(\Gamma_0,X)$. The datum has the **local QAP property** when, at each first order $n$ where the coefficientwise Slavnov identity fails, its defect has a finite-jet local four-form representative $\delta_n$ of ghost number one and a finite-jet local three-form $\rho_n$ of ghost number two satisfying
+
+$$\mathfrak S(\Gamma^{(<n)})=\lambda^n\int_U\delta_n+O(\lambda^{n+1}),\qquad B_{\Gamma_0}\delta_n+d_H\rho_n=0.$$
+
+This local representative and consistency condition are part of the QAP input; the definition does not prove the QAP. The finite-jet local BRST cohomology $H^{g,p}(s\mid d_H)$ uses the local forms, ghost grading, and horizontal differential of the preceding cohomology definition; $d_H$-exact top forms are identified with zero only under the declared compact-support or boundary condition. The coefficientwise Slavnov–Taylor identity is $\mathfrak S(\Gamma(\lambda))=0$ as an equality of formal power series.
+
