+## Example
+
+Let $U\subseteq\mathbb R^{1,3}$ be an open region with compact support or boundary conditions that remove total-derivative terms. Work in four-dimensional natural units. In the finite-jet algebra take $A_\mu$ even with ghost number $0$ and dimension $1$, $c$ odd with ghost number $1$ and dimension $0$, $\bar c$ odd with ghost number $-1$ and dimension $2$, and $b$ even with ghost number $0$ and dimension $2$. Use the Lie-algebra bracket and odd left BRST derivation
+
+$$sA_\mu=\partial_\mu c+[A_\mu,c],\qquad sc=-\tfrac12[c,c],\qquad s\bar c=b,\qquad sb=0,$$
+
+extended to jets by commuting with total derivatives. For a constant $\xi>0$, define the Lorenz-gauge fermion and its BRST-exact functional by
+
+$$\Psi_\xi=\int_U d^4x\,\langle\bar c,\partial^\mu A_\mu+\tfrac{\xi}{2}b\rangle,\qquad S_{\rm gf}:=s\Psi_\xi.$$
+
+Then
+
+$$S_{\rm gf}=\int_U d^4x\,\left(\langle b,\partial^\mu A_\mu\rangle+\tfrac{\xi}{2}\langle b,b\rangle-\langle\bar c,\partial^\mu D_\mu c\rangle\right),\qquad D_\mu c:=\partial_\mu c+[A_\mu,c],$$
+
+is a local, even, ghost-number-zero functional of density dimension $4$, and it is BRST-closed. It is an exact gauge-fixing term in the formal local algebra; this example does not claim a global representative for every gauge orbit or a continuum functional measure.
+
+## Example references
+
+[Barnich, Brandt, and Henneaux, §§2.2–2.3 and Appendix 2.A](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0002245)
+
+[Maas, §§4.5 and 4.7.2](https://static.uni-graz.at/fileadmin/_Persoenliche_Webseite/maas_axel/qftii2024.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The finite-jet non-Abelian BRST algebra, the optional nonminimal variables, and the Lorenz gauge data in the example statement.
+
+[F1] The BRST operator is an odd left derivation with $sA_\mu=\partial_\mu c+[A_\mu,c]$, $sc=-\tfrac12[c,c]$, and it commutes with total derivatives. [[def-qftx-nonabelian-brst-complex]]
+
+[F2] The minimal non-Abelian BRST differential is square-zero on the complete finite-jet algebra. [[lem-qftx-nonabelian-brst-nilpotence]]
+
+[F3] The nonminimal pair has $s\bar c=b$ and $sb=0$ in the selected BV convention. [[def-qftx-bv-antibracket-master-action]]
+
+[F4] The local cohomology quotient identifies $s$-exact ghost-number-zero four-forms with the zero class, modulo the declared horizontal exact terms. [[def-qftx-local-brst-cohomology]]
+
+## Verification
+
+**Given:** [F1]–[F4] and the compact-support or boundary condition from the statement.
+
+1.1 Since $\bar c$ is odd, the left-derivation rule gives $s\langle\bar c,\partial^\mu A_\mu\rangle=\langle b,\partial^\mu A_\mu\rangle-\langle\bar c,\partial^\mu sA_\mu\rangle$. Commutation of $s$ with total derivatives and $sA_\mu=D_\mu c$ give $\partial^\mu sA_\mu=\partial^\mu D_\mu c$. Likewise $s\langle\bar c,\xi b/2\rangle=\xi\langle b,b\rangle/2$ because $sb=0$. Adding these terms yields the displayed expression for $S_{\rm gf}$. [given, F1, F3, algebra]
+
+1.2 The displayed density is local and has dimension $2+2=4$ in the $b\partial A$ term, $2+2=4$ in the $b^2$ term, and $2+1+1+0=4$ in the $\bar c\partial D c$ term; its ghost number is zero and its parity is even. [given, algebra]
+
+2.1 The square of the extended derivation vanishes on $A,c$ by [F2] and on $\bar c,b$ since $s^2\bar c=sb=0$ and $s^2b=0$. Its square is an even derivation, so it vanishes on every finite-jet polynomial; therefore $sS_{\rm gf}=s^2\Psi_\xi=0$. Since $S_{\rm gf}=s\Psi_\xi$, [F4] identifies its local cohomology class with zero. [F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
+
